# =============================================================================
# PHASE 2 Extension C — SERVICE ISOLATION (pf rules)
# Install on Mac 3 (Backend A) and Mac 4 (Backend B) as
#     /etc/pf.anchors/cn-backend
#
# Goal from the spec: only Mac 2 (the edge) may reach backend ports 3001/3002.
# A client machine must NOT be able to connect to them directly.
#
# pf evaluates rules top-to-bottom and "quick" means FIRST MATCH WINS, so the
# pass rules MUST come before the block rules. Getting this order wrong is the
# classic pf mistake -- the block would win and even nginx would be locked out.
# =============================================================================

# 1) Always allow the machine to talk to its own backend (localhost tests,
#    `curl http://localhost:3001/...`). Without this, the backend owner can no
#    longer test their own service.
pass in quick proto tcp from 127.0.0.1 to any port 3001
pass in quick proto tcp from 127.0.0.1 to any port 3002

# 2) Allow ONLY THE EDGE TIER in. This is the whole point of the extension: the
#    backends become reachable exclusively through an edge.
#
#    Note "tier", not "machine". Mac 2 is the primary edge and Mac 4 is the
#    standby edge (Ext E). If we allowed only Mac 2, then the moment we cut the
#    DNS record over to the standby, the standby would be firewalled out of the
#    backends and would return 502 for everything -- Extension C would silently
#    break Extension E. Production works the same way: the security group allows
#    the load-balancer tier, not one specific load-balancer host.
pass in quick proto tcp from <MAC2_IP> to any port 3001
pass in quick proto tcp from <MAC2_IP> to any port 3002
pass in quick proto tcp from <MAC4_IP> to any port 3001
pass in quick proto tcp from <MAC4_IP> to any port 3002

# 3) Everyone else is dropped.
#    "block drop" discards silently (client sees a TIMEOUT), as opposed to
#    "block return" which sends RST (client sees CONNECTION REFUSED).
#    We deliberately use drop, because a silent drop is what a real firewall
#    does -- and the timeout-vs-refused distinction is a great viva question:
#    refused = nothing listening; timeout = something is filtering you.
block drop in quick proto tcp from any to any port 3001
block drop in quick proto tcp from any to any port 3002
